the pandemic changed many people's  working environment some are on a hybrid  model  others permanently working from home  remotely so how do you nurture  relationships with your colleagues  working from home and how does one  advance in one's career  in a virtual environment kirstjen joyce  caught up with an expert who says he  must be very deliberate about it  it really just became the new way of  life for us jake elson is the chief  operating officer for  sae institute north america he's based  in las vegas but oversees the operations  of half a dozen u.s campus locations and  one in british columbia  when the pandemic hit he transitioned to  fully remote  i went from visiting my teams every  other month or quarterly to not seeing  folks face to face  for several months and those in canada  now for over a year  managing an operation with more than ten  thousand students elsa needed to remain  connected  with many of us zoom happy hour seemed  to be a good way to push past the zoom  fatigue and really spend some quality  time socializing and re-establishing  relationships you have to be intentional  that's the hardest part  kimberly b cummings gives about 50  speaking engagements a year on how to  successfully advance in one's career  she says it's all about relationships  and says even in a virtual world  you still need to cultivate your network  so there are four  key relationships that every  professional needs peers teachers  mentors and sponsors  the key difference in mentors and  sponsors are that sponsors  have the influence to pull you from  where you are  to where you'd like to be sometimes  sponsors can be mentors  but not every mentor can be a sponsor  because they may not have the influence  needed  to help expedite your career it's not  too difficult to identify the power  players in your work environment  generally they're the person who speaks  at all the conferences they're normally  selected for key  projects there's generally a buzz about  them when they walk into the room  if you're unsure who that person is i  definitely would ask around  but just how do you maintain all these  different relationships in a virtual  working world  so when you've been in that zoom meeting  and you end ten minutes early  who would you have walked to go get  coffee with ask them to hop on a quick  call or slap them to keep up to date  all of the mentors who you love seeing  once a month in the elevator and you had  a great conversation  reach out to them to have a 15-minute  check-in look for all those incidental  moments  elsin says it was important for his  colleagues to incorporate some levity as  well  friday rockstar bingo sessions with some  of our campuses  we even during the holidays held you  know a virtual white elephant  we really want to just provide just some  fun and interactive sessions to stay  connected  as for networking cummings says today  it's all about deepening the existing  relationships you have  the tip that i always give folks is when  you're on a virtual event  see who is busy in the chat and figure  out  who is the most engaged who who can you  connect with  ask a question yourself that's a great  way to shine the light on yourself a  little bit during a virtual event  and then take that relationship offline  ask for someone to  meet with you for 15 minutes i'd love to  debrief with you about the event that  just happened  kirstjen joyce 8 news now cummings says  her biggest fear is that introverts will  be left behind in some aspect of virtual  work she says they will have to work  even harder  at initiating relationships and contacts
 

